                <fr:title text="HyperPlonk and the Multilinear Turn">HyperPlonk and the Multilinear Turn</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Plonkish constraints over the Boolean hypercube rather than a multiplicative subgroup, so the prover never runs an FFT. The clearest statement of what moving to <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002M/" title="Univariate and Multilinear Polynomials" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002M/" display-uri="dhsorens-002M" type="local">multilinear</fr:link> representations does and does not buy.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The translation of gate and wiring constraints into sumcheck claims.</html:li>
        <html:li>What replaces the quotient polynomial and the low-degree test.</html:li>
        <html:li>Cost profile: no FFT, more commitment work, different memory behaviour.</html:li>
        <html:li>High-degree custom gates, which this handles better than univariate Plonk.</html:li>
        <html:li>Why the field choice matters less here than in the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0037/" title="FRI" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0037/" display-uri="dhsorens-0037" type="local">FRI</fr:link> world — or whether it does.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
